# `rdec types --corpus --baseline` — the KNOWN over-truth claims of M6 type
# recovery, counted per `subject|kind` family. The families and the counts are
# produced by the scorer in `crates/cli/src/m6.rs` against `corpus/truth.tsv`
# (measured 2026-11-08 in lane m6e at the merge of master d295999).
#
# Same rule as `.gate/unwrap-baseline`: a RISE is a red build (the pass invented a
# claim it cannot witness), a FALL is red too (lower the number in this same
# commit, so the census on disk cannot drift from the census in the code), and a
# lie family with no row here is red by definition. `crates/cli/src/m6.rs`'s
# `baseline_ratchet_tests` pins all four directions.
#
# The 4 rows below are ONE family: the `carry_chain` void-return lies (O0, O1, O2,
# Oz — one per opt level; the file stem is the truth key, the function is `f`).
# `corpus/src/carry_chain.c` declares
# `void f(unsigned long a, unsigned long b, unsigned long *lo, unsigned long *hi)`;
# in all four opt levels the tail reloads a spilled slot into RAX and the LIFTER
# records `Return(value)` for a function that returns nothing. The type
# pass then faithfully reports a claim over the RAX cell — the lie is in the IR
# shape, and its owner is the lifter's void-return model, not type recovery. The
# fix that closes this family is a lifter change (record a void return when the
# tail value is a spilled slot with no reader, and have `verify` bound it); the fix
# that is NOT allowed is a scorer that stops looking at declared-void sites.
carry_chain|void-return 4
